<header class="site-header">
  <p class="eyebrow">An open reference for understanding parametric font design</p>
  <h1>Parametric source design</h1>
  <p>    
    Variable fonts make it possible to interpolate between many design states.
    Parametric source design asks a more specific question: which individual
    transformations make up those states, and how can each one be controlled
    clearly and consistently?
  </p>
</header>

<div class="intro-content">
  <section>
    <h2>What this archive is for</h2>

    <p>
      This documentation examines the design reasoning behind parametric
      sources. It is intended for type designers who understand interpolation
      and want to study how a typeface can be organised around per parameter transformations.
    </p>

    <p>
      Rather than treating a source as a complete named style, the archive
      considers the individual changes from which a system is made and the
      decisions needed to make them work across a typeface.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What it records</h2>

    <p>
      This is an open research archive. It records the intended behaviour of
      parametric transformations alongside the evidence used to evaluate them:
      selected glyph constructions, related adjustments, exceptions, source
      data, mappings, and proofing specimens.
    </p>

    <p>
      The aim is not to present parameters as fixed recipes, but to make their
      design logic available for inspection, comparison, and further research.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Explore the documentation</h2>

    <p>
      The archive is focused on describig the design specifics of the parametric sources.
      It is separated into two sections: Explanation and Reference. The Explanation
      pages are articles that contextualise the reference material and allow to better understand the
      roles and placement of each piece of information in the parametric source system.
      The Reference pages are specific documented examples of the way the system is implemented.
    </p>

    <ul>
      <li>
        <a href="/explanation/">Explanation</a> examines the concepts and
        reasoning behind parametric source systems. This is the <em> how? </em>
      </li>
      <li>
        <a href="/reference/">Reference</a> provides the documented projects,
        axes, mappings, source data, and proofing evidence. This is the <em> what? </em>
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Current scope</h2>

    <p>
      The first documented project is Amstelvar Avar2. Computer Modern and
      Roboto Delta are intended as later projects and points of comparison.
    </p>

    <p>
      The archive will expand as further projects and findings become available.
    </p>
  </section>
</div>